1: # P3F.26. The component-supported OS49 sphere construction
2: 
3: **Proved.** The rational elliptic surface
4: \[
5:  y^2=x^3-3t^3(t-1)x+2t^4(t-1)(t-\tfrac12)
6:                                                         \tag{P3F.26.1}
7: \]
8: has fibres \(IV^*,II,I_2\) at \(0,1,\infty\), and primitive
9: Mordell--Weil generator \(G=(t^2,t^2)\). Put
10: \(M=\mathcal O(G-O)\), and suspend translation by \(-Q=-3G\).
11: There is a linearization whose divisor is the identity component
12: \(C_0\) of the \(I_2\) fibre, with multiplicity one. Its primitive
13: rank-two toric filling at infinity, the original line-complement
14: quotient at \(II\), and the order-three free logarithmic cap at
15: \(IV^*\), with logarithmic vector \((1,-1,0,0)^t\), glue to a
16: smooth compact complex manifold diffeomorphic to \(S^6\). Its
17: integral topology is computed below, including every differential.
18: This is the component-supported target specified in the prompt;
19: we do not identify its linearization with a whole-fibre divisor.
20: 
21: ## The source, section and component divisor
22: 
23: Write \(f=t^3(t-1)\), \(g=t^4(t-1)(t-1/2)\). Direct calculation gives
24: \[
25:  f^3-g^2=-\tfrac14t^8(t-1)^2,
26:  \qquad j/1728=4t(1-t).
27: \]
28: The vanishing orders at zero and one are respectively
29: \((3,4,8)\) and \((1,1,2)\) for \((f,g,\Delta)\).
30: At infinity the minimal coefficients are units and the discriminant
31: has order two. These give exactly the asserted Kodaira fibres.
32: Substitution verifies the displayed section. On the cubic root
33: \(t=s^3\), set \(x=s^4X,y=s^6Y\). The central good-reduction
34: curve is \(Y^2=X^3+1\), and \(G\) specializes to \((0,1)\), a
35: nonzero point of order three. At infinity, with \(q=1/t\), its
36: coordinates \((q^2x,q^3y)=(1,q)\) meet the Weierstrass node
37: \((1,0)\), and the first resolution gives component one of
38: \(I_2\). Its intersections with \(O\) are empty. The height is
39: \[
40:  2-\frac43-\frac12=\frac16.
41: \]
42: Shioda--Tate gives rank one. The verified Oguiso--Shioda Main
43: Theorem table used in P3F.1 has exactly one row with root lattice
44: \(E_6+A_1\), namely row49, with trivial torsion and primitive
45: height \(1/6\). Since multiplication of a section multiplies its
46: height by the square of that integer, the displayed \(G\) is a
47: primitive generator. This application has the required rationality:
48: the Weierstrass fundamental line has degree one, the base is
49: \(\mathbb P^1\), and the section excludes multiple fibres.
50: 
51: For translation by \(-Q\), the divisor of the line of
52: linearizations is
53: \[
54:  D=G-O-(-2G)+(-3G).
55: \]
56: At \(IV^*\), \(Q\) meets the identity component, so all
57: multidegrees cancel. At \(I_2\), its multidegrees on \((C_0,C_1)\)
58: are \((-2,2)\). They equal those of \(C_0\), since
59: \(C_0^2=-2\) and \(C_0C_1=2\). On a rational elliptic surface
60: the difference \(D-C_0\), having zero generic degree and zero
61: component multidegrees, is an integral fibre class. Its intersection
62: with \(O\) is zero: the height formula gives
63: \((-2G)O=(-3G)O=0\), and \(DO=1=C_0O\).
64: Thus \(D\sim C_0\). Choose its canonical section and multiply
65: it by a small nonzero scalar \(\lambda\). This specifies the
66: linearization and its exact zero divisor.
67: 
68: ## Actual local fillings and their gluing
69: 
70: Near \(I_2\), use a Tate coordinate \(Z\) on the source and a
71: nonzero line coordinate \(V\). A logarithmic representative of
72: \(G\) has valuation one. Inverting \(G\) changes every associated
73: marking coherently; choose the sign giving that positive valuation.
74: The two commuting deck periods have valuations
75: \[
76:  h:(Z,V)\longmapsto(q^2aZ,qbV),\qquad
77:  g:(Z,V)\longmapsto(q^{-3}cZ,\lambda q^{-1}dV),       \tag{P3F.26.2}
78: \]
79: where the four coefficients are nonvanishing holomorphic units.
80: To check the second valuation without a guessed toric model,
81: translation by \(-Q\) exchanges \(C_0\) and \(C_1\).
82: The norm of the linearization has divisor
83: \(C_0+C_1=F\). Therefore \(h^3g^2\), whose first valuation
84: is zero, has second valuation one. This gives exactly the exponent
85: \(-1\) in (P3F.26.2).
86: 
87: The integral change of deck generators
88: \[
89:  H=h^{-1}g^{-1},\qquad K=h^3g^2
90: \]
91: has determinant one, and its valuation columns are respectively
92: \((1,0)^t,(0,1)^t\). Thus the precise local filling is the
93: unit-square Mumford model triangulated by its diagonal, with units
94: retained in the deck maps. For a triangle with lattice vertices
95: \((i,j),(i+1,j),(i,j+1)\), the cone on the three height-one rays
96: has determinant one; its affine chart is \(\mathbb C^3\), with
97: \(q\) the product of the three toric coordinates. The other
98: triangle has the analogous chart. Adjacent charts glue by the
99: integral monomials determined by their common rays. Translations
100: by \(H,K\) preserve the fan and multiply those monomials by
101: holomorphic units. Modulo this full period lattice there are one
102: vertex, three edges and two triangles. The usual annular moduli
103: fundamental domain in \((\log|Z|,\log|V|)\), together with these
104: boundary charts, is compact over a closed sufficiently small disc.
105: It proves properness and the Hausdorff property. The unimodular
106: charts prove smoothness. The central normalization is the standard
107: hexagonal toric surface, with opposite boundary curves identified
108: by the retained unit periods. Its Euler number is two.
109: 
110: At the type \(II\) fibre the linearization is invertible. Use
111: the actual smooth total line complement \(M^\times\) over the
112: original elliptic neighbourhood, divided by the contracting lift
113: of \(-Q\). A Hermitian norm and a sufficiently small scalar
114: make that lift strictly decrease the norm, uniformly on this compact
115: neighbourhood. Its iterates therefore act freely and properly
116: discontinuously, with a compact annular fundamental domain. The
117: quotient is smooth, proper over the base disc, and agrees with the
118: regular torus family on the punctured disc. No replacement of this
119: additive source fibre is made.
120: 
121: At \(IV^*\), cubic good reduction gives a smooth elliptic family.
122: The point \(Q=3G\) specializes to zero. The degree-zero line and
123: the nonvanishing linearization consequently give a smooth two-torus
124: family on the root chart. The linear homology deck matrix is
125: \(\widehat T_A=(T_A^{-1})^t\), displayed below. The vector
126: \(\ell=(1,-1,0,0)^t\) is invariant. The affine cyclic action is
127: translation by \(\ell/3\) followed by \(\widehat T_A\).
128: The invariant gamma covector evaluates to one on \(\ell\),
129: so its nonidentity powers have gamma displacement \(1/3\) and
130: \(2/3\), and are free. Its quotient is the smooth finite cap.
131: Its punctured gluing is the holomorphic logarithmic translation
132: \[
133:  (z,s)\longmapsto
134:  \left(z+\frac{\log s}{2\pi i}\Pi(s)\ell,s\right),
135:  \qquad t=s^3.
136: \]
137: Changing the branch of the logarithm adds an integral period;
138: the cubic deck changes it by the displayed affine translation.
139: This verifies descent and gives its inverse by subtraction.
140: 
141: Glue these models to the regular family over the three-punctured
142: sphere by these logarithmic and toric chart identifications.
143: On every compact parameter subannulus the discs can be shrunk once
144: to give uniform compact fundamental domains. Their union with a
145: compact regular-base part proves compactness of each threefold and
146: properness of the holomorphic family over that subannulus. Exhaust
147: the punctured parameter disc by such subannuli; the constructions
148: agree on their common ordinary locus and by the same toric charts,
149: so they glue to the family on the whole punctured disc. This does
150: not impose a parameter-independent cusp radius at \(\lambda=0\).
151: Smoothness, holomorphy and the Hausdorff property follow from the
152: explicit local charts and their annular overlap maps.
153: 
154: ## Marked monodromy and its completeness
155: 
156: The elliptic cohomology matrices can be marked
157: \[
158:  A=\begin{pmatrix}-2&-3\\1&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
159:  B=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
160:  C=\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad ABC=I.
161: \]
162: This is a marking of the displayed surface. Its exact degree-two
163: map \(j/1728=4t(1-t)\) has permutations \(S=(12)\) over one,
164: \(R=1\) over zero, and cusp permutation \((12)\). The associated
165: index-two modular cover therefore has two order-three elliptic
166: points and one cusp of width two. Taking the local lifts of types
167: \(IV^*\) and \(II\) gives the displayed order-three and order-six
168: matrices, with the positive cusp transvection \(C\). There is
169: no additional quadratic twist: an odd valuation changes one of
170: these fixed local types, and a rational function on \(\mathbb P^1\)
171: with all valuations even is a square.
172: 
173: There is also no unverified integral choice of the section cocycle.
174: Since \(B-I\) is unimodular, choose the unique Abel--Jacobi lift
175: which makes its \(B\)-cocycle zero. The \(I_2\) component implies
176: the remaining cocycle is \((r,0)\), with \(r\) odd. The product
177: relation gives the \(A\)-cocycle \((-r,0)\). For an elliptic
178: matrix \(E=(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{smallmatrix})\),
179: cocycle \((j,h)\), and scalar entry \(z\), the line/suspension
180: weights \((1,3)\) give
181: \[
182:  T(E,(j,h),z)=
183:  \begin{pmatrix}
184:  1&3(cj-ah)&3(dj-bh)&z\\
185:  0&a&b&j\\0&c&d&h\\0&0&0&1
186:  \end{pmatrix}.
187: \]
188: The order-three condition gives \(z_A=-r^2\).
189: The norm calculation above gives
190: \(z_C=(3r^2-1)/2\). The central entry of the global product is
191: \((r^2-1)/2\). Its only integral zeros are \(r=\pm1\),
192: interchanged by \(G\mapsto-G\). Hence, in the positive marking,
193: the actual cohomological tuple is
194: \[
195:  T_A=\begin{pmatrix}1&-3&-3&-1\\0&-2&-3&-1\\0&1&1&0\\0&0&0&1\end{pmatrix},
196:  \qquad
197:  T_B=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0&0\\0&1&1&0\\0&-1&0&0\\0&0&0&1\end{pmatrix},
198: \]
199: \[
200:  T_C=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&3&1\\0&1&2&1\\0&0&1&0\\0&0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.
201:                                                         \tag{P3F.26.3}
202: \]
203: Their product and \(T_A^3\) are the identity. These and the
204: normal-function normalization equation are executed exactly in
205: `code/part3/os49_component.py`.
206: 
207: ## Integral topology
208: 
209: The local type \(II\) neighbourhood retracts onto its cuspidal
210: rational curve, which is homeomorphic to \(S^2\): its normalization
211: is a continuous bijection from a compact space to a Hausdorff space.
212: The restriction of \(M\) has degree zero and is topologically
213: trivial. A local logarithm of \(Q\) gives a translation isotopy
214: through its identity-component group; on the central cuspidal
215: curve this is its additive translation, fixing the cusp. Its
216: suspension, including the line, thus retracts onto
217: \(S^2\times T^2\). The collapse from a smooth fibre kills the
218: two elliptic one-cycles and has degree one on its oriented two-cell.
219: It follows integrally that its specialization image is the complete
220: invariant lattice of \(\bigwedge^qT_B\) in every degree. At
221: the rank-two cusp the unimodular fan calculation is exactly the
222: normalization/double-curve complex of E26 Lemma7.4: the checked
223: period basis has index one, so its specialization also has full
224: integral invariant image. Its two vanishing circles are primitive.
225: 
226: In van Kampen, these two nonfinite fillings kill the section
227: meridians and the images of \(\widehat T_B-I,\widehat T_C-I\).
228: The eight-column relation matrix has Smith factors \((1,1,1)\);
229: its quotient is generated by the invariant gamma direction.
230: The base relation kills the remaining finite meridian, and its
